`shared_iter` + `iter_step` handles re-entrancy. If the reader is created via gen_iter with It::Gen, iter_step would call g.next() while holding borrow... For It::Gen, g.next() -> f() user code while borrow held. Better create reader via shared_iter(|| ...) so iter_step drops borrow for It::Shared. But the closure captures `it` (the source iterator) by &mut? shared_iter takes Fn() so we need the source iterator in an Rc<RefCell<It>>, then the closure steps it via iter_step (which handles re-entrancy) or borrow. Let me see how existing code builds shared csv readers... Current csv_fn reader uses `let mut it = iter(&src)?; gen_iter(move || Ok(csv_record(&mut it, &d)?...))`. This holds `it` mutably in the closure - if the source iterator's step runs user code, no RefCell borrow is held over it? `csv_record(&mut it, ...)` calls `it.next()` while the closure is running; the closure itself isn't borrowing anything else. It::Gen is invoked via g.next() with g borrow held, but g is the reader's own It::Gen; the user code runs inside f, and it holds no RefCell borrow (except if it.next()... the source `it` is owned by the closure, not borrowed). Actually the issue rule: "Never hold a RefCell borrow across anything that can run user code". In It::Gen, when g.next() is called, g is a borrow of the outer cell; inside f() user code could re-enter the same reader -> try_borrow would... gen_iter uses It::Gen, iter_step falls to `_ => g.next()` with g borrowed. If user code re-enters the same iterator, the outer cell is already mutably borrowed by g.next() call -> RefCell panic, not a clean ValueError. The rule specifically says don't hold borrow across user code. But that's existing code for csv.reader; not my concern unless I reuse it.

